# 5. EP + BVA – Paytm Amount Field

I selected the **recharge amount** field in a Paytm-style app.

**Assumption:** For this example, the allowed recharge amount is **₹10 to ₹5,000**.

## A. Equivalence Partitioning

| Partition              |         Amount Range | Example | Expected Result |
| ---------------------- | -------------------: | ------: | --------------- |
| **P1 – Below range**   |        Less than ₹10 |      ₹5 | ❌ Rejected      |
| **P2 – Valid range**   |           ₹10–₹5,000 |    ₹500 | ✅ Accepted      |
| **P3 – Above range**   |     More than ₹5,000 |  ₹6,000 | ❌ Rejected      |
| **P4 – Invalid input** | Letters/special text |   `ABC` | ❌ Rejected      |
| **P5 – Blank**         |            No amount |   Empty | ❌ Rejected      |

## B. Boundary Value Analysis

| Test Case ID | Amount | Expected Result |
| ------------ | -----: | --------------- |
| **TC-A01**   |     ₹9 | ❌ Rejected      |
| **TC-A02**   |    ₹10 | ✅ Accepted      |
| **TC-A03**   |    ₹11 | ✅ Accepted      |
| **TC-A04**   | ₹4,999 | ✅ Accepted      |
| **TC-A05**   | ₹5,000 | ✅ Accepted      |
| **TC-A06**   | ₹5,001 | ❌ Rejected      |

### Why use both EP and BVA?

**Equivalence Partitioning** helps divide the possible input values into groups, so we do not have to test every amount. **Boundary Value Analysis** focuses specifically on the values at and around the limits, where mistakes commonly occur.

**In simple words:** EP checks the different **categories of input**, while BVA checks the **edges of those categories**.
